### obscura version or commit

v0.2.4

### OS and architecture

Windows 11 Pro x64 (build 26200)

### How are you using obscura?

Other (CDP server via Rod)

### Affected area

JavaScript or DOM behavior

### Build variant

Render-enabled with stealth (official stealth archive or --features render,stealth)

### Stealth mode

Same result with it on and off

### Reproduction

Run this on https://example.com with `Runtime.evaluate` and `awaitPromise: true`:

```js
new Promise((resolve) => {
  const out = {};
  window.__cb = (w) => { out.callback = w && w.document ? "called with the frame window" : String(w); };
  const a = document.createElement("iframe");
  a.src = 'javascript:"<p id=x>hello</p>"';
  const b = document.createElement("iframe");
  b.src = "javascript:(parent.__cb(window), '')";
  document.body.append(a, b);
  setTimeout(() => {
    try {
      out.html = a.contentDocument && a.contentDocument.body ? a.contentDocument.body.innerHTML : null;
    } catch (e) {
      out.html = "error: " + e.message;
    }
    out.callback = out.callback || "not called";
    resolve(out);
  }, 1500);
})
```

### Expected behavior

`{"callback":"called with the frame window","html":"<p id=\"x\">hello</p>"}`, which is what headless Chrome returns.

### Actual behavior

`{"callback":"not called","html":""}`. The `javascript:` URL is never run, so the frame stays empty and the callback never fires.

On a real page that builds frames like this while loading, `Page.navigate` also returns `Network error: Forbidden URL scheme 'javascript' - only http, https, and file are allowed` even though the main document loads fine.

### Does headless Chrome behave the same?

No: headless Chrome renders or behaves correctly and obscura does not

