### obscura version or commit

v0.2.4

### OS and architecture

Windows 11 Pro x64 (build 26200)

### How are you using obscura?

Other (CDP server via Rod)

### Affected area

JavaScript or DOM behavior

### Build variant

Render-enabled with stealth (official stealth archive or --features render,stealth)

### Stealth mode

Same result with it on and off

### Reproduction

Run this on any page with `Runtime.evaluate` and `awaitPromise: true`:

```js
new Promise((resolve) => {
  const events = [];
  for (const [rel, as] of [["stylesheet"], ["preload", "style"]]) {
    const link = document.createElement("link");
    link.rel = rel;
    if (as) link.as = as;
    link.href = "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/water.css@2/out/water.css?" + rel;
    link.onload = () => events.push(rel + ": load");
    link.onerror = () => events.push(rel + ": error");
    document.head.appendChild(link);
  }
  setTimeout(() => resolve(events), 3000);
})
```

### Expected behavior

`["preload: load", "stylesheet: load"]`, which is what headless Chrome returns.

### Actual behavior

`["stylesheet: load"]`. The preload link never fires `load` or `error`.

This breaks React 19 / Next.js app router pages that lazy load a component with CSS. React adds a `<link rel="preload" as="style">` for the stylesheet and waits for it to load, so the Suspense boundary never resolves and the component never mounts.

### Does headless Chrome behave the same?

No: headless Chrome renders or behaves correctly and obscura does not

The `navigator.geolocation` shim answers with a position even when the geolocation permission has never been decided. In a real Chrome that combination is impossible, so two lines of JS from any page are enough to tell the browser apart.

## Repro

```bash
obscura serve --port 9333 &
node -e "
const p = require('puppeteer-core');
(async () => {
  const b = await p.connect({ browserWSEndpoint: 'ws://127.0.0.1:9333/devtools/browser' });
  const pg = await b.newPage();
  await pg.goto('https://example.com');
  await pg.evaluate(() => {
    window.__perm = 'pending';
    navigator.permissions.query({ name: 'geolocation' }).then(s => { window.__perm = s.state; });
    window.__geo = 'pending';
    navigator.geolocation.getCurrentPosition(
      p => { window.__geo = 'ok ' + p.coords.latitude + ',' + p.coords.longitude; },
      e => { window.__geo = 'error code=' + e.code; });
  });
  await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, 4000));
  console.log('permission:', await pg.evaluate(() => window.__perm));
  console.log('position  :', await pg.evaluate(() => window.__geo));
  b.disconnect(); process.exit(0);
})()"
```

## Observed (0.2.4, aarch64, Debian 12)

```
permission: prompt
position  : ok 50.125613186622765,8.687649896009162
```

## Expected

`permissions.query` reports `prompt`, i.e. nothing has been granted yet, so the call must not be able to produce a position. Chrome fails it:

```
permission: prompt
position  : error code=1
```

`code=1` is `PERMISSION_DENIED` ("User denied Geolocation" in Chromium's message). That is the whole test:

```js
navigator.permissions.query({ name: 'geolocation' }).then(s => {
  if (s.state !== 'granted') {
    navigator.geolocation.getCurrentPosition(
      () => { /* headless/spoofed: impossible in a real browser */ },
      () => { /* what Chrome does */ });
  }
});
```

## A/B, same host, same page, same exit IP

| signal | obscura 0.2.4 | Chromium (system build) |
|---|---|---|
| `permissions.query({name:'geolocation'})` | `prompt` | `prompt` |
| `getCurrentPosition` | **`ok` — 50.12,8.68** | **`error code=1`** |

Note the shim does seem to look at the origin: on a `data:` URL (opaque origin) neither callback ever fires, and `permissions.query` stays `prompt`. On an ordinary https origin it answers.

## Why it matters

Geolocation spoofing is usually invisible to the page, because a site has no way to know whether the coordinates are real. The permission state gives it one: a position that arrives without the permission ever being granted is a self-contradiction. Anti-fraud SDKs check exactly this kind of cross-surface consistency — it is the same class of tell as `Intl` disagreeing with `Date`, which #228 already fixed.

Making the shim honour the permission state (invoke the error callback with `PERMISSION_DENIED` while it is not `granted`) would close it. If the intent is to keep returning a position for convenience, gating it behind `granted` at least keeps `OBSCURA_GEOLOCATION` usable via `Browser.grantPermissions`.

I understand `OBSCURA_GEOLOCATION` is configurable by design and that a `Europe/Berlin`-based default is intentional (#228, #276) — this is not a request to change the default, and the coordinates themselves are a separate, already-configurable matter. The report is only about the impossible `(prompt, position)` pair.

Building and querying 20,000 elements from page JS takes about 850 ms on `main` (0cdd42e), against about 150 ms in Chromium and 120 ms in Lightpanda on the same machine.

Per-command timing inside `op_dom` shows the native side is cheap: `create_element` is about 0.6 us, `append_child` about 1 us. Most of the cost is in `bootstrap.js` and in crossing the bridge:

- every `Element` constructor builds a `CSSStyleDeclaration` and a `Proxy`, even when `.style` is never read;
- the document-wide MutationObserver installed for IntersectionObserver geometry is always attached, so every DOM write builds a mutation record and walks the target's ancestors, even on pages with no IntersectionObserver;
- `appendChild` runs two subtree selector queries (script preparation, window named access) plus three `isConnected` checks;
- scalar reads (`nodeType`, `getAttribute`, `tagName`, `textContent`) go through string results and `JSON.parse`.

I have a branch that addresses these with the same observable behavior; PR to follow.

On a 12,000-node page (9,000 links, every row a `<div>` with a button), the first geometry read after load takes about 1.6 s in the render build on `main` (0cdd42e) and adds about 170 MB RSS.

A phase breakdown of `layout_dom_once` puts about 60% of the time in `build()` (DOM to taffy tree plus text shaping): 15,001 inline items and 18,000 word leaves. Allocation is a large share of it:

- each word leaf clones the full `LayoutStyle` (2,760 bytes) to change one field;
- shaped lines and buffers keep spare `Vec` capacity after shaping;
- finalized inline buffers keep their shaped glyphs (112 bytes per `ShapeGlyph`), which are not needed again unless the line is laid out again.

#1111 already caches shaping plans, which covers the per-word `ShapePlan` compile; this issue is about the memory side. PR to follow.

On live sites, `Page.navigate` on `main` (0cdd42e) takes noticeably longer than in Chromium, while local fixtures load fast. There is no fixed sleep in the navigation path; the time is network round trips, and three things add round trips:

- reqwest is built without its `http2` feature (no `h2` in `Cargo.lock`), so every parallel subresource opens its own TCP and TLS connection;
- stylesheets and scripts are fetched in two waves, one after the other;
- no script runs until the slowest script has downloaded. On react.dev, execution started about 960 ms after `execute_scripts` because a 476 KB async analytics script was the slowest.

I have a branch that fixes these; PR to follow.

## Summary

In `op_fetch_url` (`crates/obscura-js/src/ops.rs`), a CDP interceptor's `Continue` URL override is only run through the SSRF gate `validate_fetch_url` inside an `if let Ok(parsed) = url::Url::parse(&new_url)`. When parsing fails, validation is silently skipped and the raw override string is still used:

```rust
let url = if let Some(new_url) = override_url {
    if let Ok(parsed) = url::Url::parse(&new_url) {
        if let Err(reason) = validate_fetch_url(&parsed, allow_private_network) {
            return Ok(/* blocked */);
        }
    }
    new_url            // <- used even when parse failed, unvalidated
} else {
    url
};
```

AGENTS.md requires a Continue URL rewrite to stay behind `validate_fetch_url` (the same SSRF gate as the original request and redirects). A parse failure bypasses that gate architecturally.

## Impact

Low in practice: `new_url` and reqwest use the same `url` crate, so a string that fails `Url::parse` here also fails in the client, which is a practical backstop. Still, the invariant is violated and the code should fail closed. Requires a CDP interceptor (operator-controlled).

## Fix

When the override URL fails to parse, block the request (return the blocked response) instead of falling through to use the raw string.

Severity: Low. Found in a security audit pass.

## Summary

The **in-page `WebSocket` constructor is a stub**: constructing a socket fires `open` and moves `readyState` to `OPEN` without dialling anything, and `send()` returns without error while silently discarding the frame. Nothing ever reaches the network.

Because `open` fires, callers believe they are connected; because neither `error` nor `close` ever fires, their reconnect loops never run either. Any page whose boot depends on a WebSocket therefore fails **silently and permanently**, and feature detection cannot catch it (`typeof WebSocket !== "undefined"` passes, `onopen` fires).

## Environment

- `obscura 0.2.2`, linux x64, sha256 `c1b4548e36549a0228c39c1cc842df425bc7253af2b0a56bd2a538d8ff7e3406`
- Ubuntu, kernel `6.8.0-139-generic`, node `24.18.0`
- Launched as `obscura serve --port <P> --host 127.0.0.1 --allow-private-network` with `OBSCURA_ALLOW_PRIVATE_NETWORK=1`, `OBSCURA_SCRIPT_DEADLINE_MS=120000`, `OBSCURA_MODULE_BUDGET_MS=30000`, driven over CDP by `puppeteer-core`
- Control: `chrome-headless-shell 153.0.8010.52` (`Google Chrome for Testing 153.0.8010.52`) on the same host, same page, same 6 s window

## Conclusive evidence: `open` fires with nothing listening

This needs no server at all — construct a WebSocket pointed at a port with nothing behind it:

```js
const t0 = performance.now();
const ws = new WebSocket("ws://127.0.0.1:4399");   // nothing is listening on 4399
ws.addEventListener("open", () => console.log("open after", performance.now() - t0, "ms", ws.readyState));
ws.addEventListener("error", () => console.log("error"));
ws.addEventListener("close", (e) => console.log("close", e.code));
```

| | Obscura 0.2.2 | chrome-headless-shell 153 |
|---|---|---|
| `open` event | **fires, 1 ms** | never fires |
| `readyState` after | **1 (`OPEN`)** | stays 0 (`CONNECTING`) |
| `error` / `close` | **neither, ever** | `error`, then `close` 1006 |
| `send()` | succeeds, discards | throws `InvalidStateError` |

A real engine cannot resolve a socket to `OPEN` when nothing accepts the connection. That row alone isolates the defect to the engine's WebSocket implementation rather than to networking, proxying or the target server.

## Server-side confirmation

With the same page also opening a WebSocket to a **live** `ws` server (`:4398`) and doing `fetch("http://127.0.0.1:4398/events")`, and the server recording every `upgrade` / `connection` / `message` / `close` / `error`:

| page probe | Obscura 0.2.2 | chrome-headless-shell 153 |
|---|---|---|
| `open` on the **live** ws | fires at 1 ms | fires at 11 ms |
| what the server received | **`[]` — zero upgrades, zero connections, zero messages** | `upgrade` + `connection` + `message` (`{"type":"hello",...}`) |
| `open` on the **dead** port | **fires at 1 ms** | never fires; `error` → `close` 1006 |
| `fetch()` to the live server | `ok:2` | `ok:2` |

So HTTP in the page's runtime is fine (the `fetch` reaches the server) while `WebSocket` is inert. Both engines report `readyState 0` immediately after the constructor returns, so Obscura's fake `open` is dispatched from a task rather than synchronously — it just never corresponds to a connection.

## Real-world impact — two independent clients, same constructor

This is not a synthetic-only problem. Two unrelated clients that boot-bind a socket behave identically under Obscura, and both fail silently:

- **react-ui-bridge**: the page logs `[react-ui-bridge] connected to ws://127.0.0.1:4317` (that line is emitted from the `open` handler), the relay reports a healthy headless session and viewport, and the app **never registers** — no `app connected` log line, `apps: []` forever. With `--engine chrome` on the same host and the same arguments the relay logs `app connected: <app> (http://…)` immediately.
- **Vite's HMR client**: logs `[vite] connecting...` and never `[vite] connected.`

The failed handshake is indistinguishable from success on the page side, so this reads as a bug in the consumer rather than in the engine — that is the expensive part.

## Minimal reproduction

Two files, ~20 s, no framework and no dev server.

`server.mjs` — HTTP + `ws` on one port, plus a page that probes both sockets and `fetch`:

```js
import { createServer } from "node:http";
import { WebSocketServer } from "ws";

const PORT = 4398, DEAD_PORT = 4399, events = [];

const page = `<!doctype html><body><pre id="out"></pre>
<script type="module">
const rec = (k, v) => { document.getElementById("out").textContent += k + ": " + JSON.stringify(v) + "\\n"; };
window.__probe = { events: [], diag: {} };
window.__probe.diag.fetch = "pending";
fetch("http://127.0.0.1:${PORT}/events").then(r => r.text())
  .then(t => { window.__probe.diag.fetch = "ok:" + t.length; })
  .catch(e => { window.__probe.diag.fetch = "fail:" + e; });
const t0 = performance.now();
const dead = new WebSocket("ws://127.0.0.1:${DEAD_PORT}");   // nothing listening
dead.addEventListener("open", () => {
  window.__probe.diag.deadOpen = { fired: true, ms: Math.round(performance.now() - t0), readyState: dead.readyState };
});
dead.addEventListener("error", () => { window.__probe.diag.deadError = true; });
dead.addEventListener("close", (e) => { window.__probe.diag.deadClose = e.code; });

const ws = new WebSocket("ws://127.0.0.1:${PORT}");
ws.addEventListener("open", () => { rec("open", ws.readyState); ws.send(JSON.stringify({ type: "hello" })); });
ws.addEventListener("message", (e) => rec("message", String(e.data)));
</script></body>`;

const http = createServer((req, res) => {
  if (req.url === "/events") { res.writeHead(200, {"content-type":"application/json"}); return res.end(JSON.stringify(events)); }
  res.writeHead(200, { "content-type": "text/html" }); res.end(page);
});
const wss = new WebSocketServer({ server: http });
wss.on("headers", (_h, req) => events.push({ event: "upgrade", url: req.url }));
wss.on("connection", (s) => {
  events.push({ event: "connection" });
  s.on("message", (d) => { events.push({ event: "message", data: String(d) }); s.send(JSON.stringify({ type: "ack" })); });
});
http.listen(PORT, "127.0.0.1", () => console.log("listening"));
```

Drive it with `puppeteer-core` (Obscura: `puppeteer.connect({ browserURL: "http://127.0.0.1:<cdp port>" })`; control: `puppeteer.launch({ executablePath: <chrome-headless-shell>, args: ["--no-sandbox"] })`), `page.goto("http://127.0.0.1:4398/")`, wait ~6 s, then read `window.__probe` and `GET /events`.

Obscura result:
```json
{ "diag": { "fetch": "ok:2", "deadOpen": { "fired": true, "ms": 1, "readyState": 1 } },
  "events": [["open", 1], ["sent", true]], "server": [] }
```
chrome result:
```json
{ "diag": { "fetch": "ok:2", "deadError": true, "deadClose": 1006 },
  "events": [["open", 1], ["message", "{\"type\":\"ack\"}"]],
  "server": [{"event":"upgrade"},{"event":"connection"},{"event":"message","data":"{\"type\":\"hello\"}"}] }
```

## Suggested fix

Implement the client `WebSocket` in the JS runtime.

If that is out of scope for now, please make the stub **honest** — either throw from the constructor (`new WebSocket(...)` → `Error: WebSocket is not implemented`) or remain in `CONNECTING` and never fire `open`. Firing `open` against no connection is strictly worse than not implementing the API: it converts a loud, immediately diagnosable failure into a silent one, defeats every feature-detection idiom (`typeof WebSocket`, `onopen`), and disables the caller's own error and reconnect paths.

## Related

- #851 — Worker missing `XMLHttpRequest` ("There is no supported http request object")
- #592 — iframe `contentWindow` missing almost all globals
- #63 — `Element.prototype.elementFromPoint` not implemented

Same family (unimplemented web platform API), but this one is in the **main world** rather than a secondary context, so it takes down the page's own bootstrap rather than a third-party script.

`parseFromString` looks like it ignores the mime type completely — valid XML comes back as an HTML document, root `<html>` in the XHTML namespace.

I saw #504 and that the scope there was the malformed-XML/parsererror case, with full XML semantics explicitly left out, so this may already be known. Filing it anyway because the practical damage isn't tag-case, it's that the root element and its namespace are wrong, so anything that inspects them gives up immediately.

0.2.2, `obscura-aarch64-macos`:

```bash
obscura fetch https://example.com -e "
(() => {
  const P = new DOMParser();
  const probe = (src, mime) => {
    const d = P.parseFromString(src, mime);
    const e = d.documentElement;
    return { mime, root: e && e.nodeName, localName: e && e.localName, ns: e && e.namespaceURI };
  };
  return JSON.stringify([
    probe('<Edmx xmlns=\"http://docs.oasis-open.org/odata/ns/edmx\" Version=\"4.0\"><DataServices/></Edmx>', 'application/xml'),
    probe('<root><child/></root>', 'text/xml'),
    probe('<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><rect/></svg>', 'image/svg+xml'),
    probe('<p>hi</p>', 'text/html')
  ], null, 1);
})()"
```

All four come back identical:

```json
[
 { "mime": "application/xml", "root": "HTML", "localName": "html", "ns": "http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" },
 { "mime": "text/xml",        "root": "HTML", "localName": "html", "ns": "http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" },
 { "mime": "image/svg+xml",   "root": "HTML", "localName": "html", "ns": "http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" },
 { "mime": "text/html",       "root": "HTML", "localName": "html", "ns": "http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" }
]
```

Chrome returns `Edmx` / `root` / `svg` with the right namespaces for the first three.

The `image/svg+xml` row is maybe worth a look on its own — #485 fixed SVG parsed from markup staying in the SVG namespace, but through `parseFromString` it's still XHTML `<html>`.

What I hit this with: a SAP UI5 + CAP OData v4 app that renders fine in Chrome doesn't start at all. UI5 parses the OData `$metadata` through DOMParser and checks the root:

```
/odata/v4/<Service>/$metadata: expected <Edmx> in namespace 'http://docs.oasis-open.org/odata/ns/edmx'
```

and then its declarative `*.view.xml` views fail the same way, showing up as `TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'childNodes')` while rendering routing targets. Page load and JS execution are otherwise fine.

Tag case does bite here as a side effect — EDMX is case-sensitive (`Edmx`, `DataServices`, `EntityContainer`) and the HTML parser lowercases everything.

`document.elementFromPoint()` appears to resolve overlapping positioned siblings by DOM order rather than paint order.

Minimal repro:

```html
<style>
.dialog { position: fixed; top: 100px; left: 100px; width: 400px; height: 200px; z-index: 1000; }
.close-button { position: absolute; top: 10px; right: 10px; width: 32px; height: 32px; z-index: 1002; }
.loading-overlay { position: absolute; inset: 0; z-index: 1001; }
</style>
<div class="dialog">
  <button class="close-button">×</button>
  <div class="loading-overlay"></div>
</div>
<script>
const button = document.querySelector('.close-button');
const rect = button.getBoundingClientRect();
const hit = document.elementFromPoint(rect.left + rect.width / 2, rect.top + rect.height / 2);
console.log(hit.className);
</script>
```

The button is earlier in the dialog DOM and has `z-index: 1002`; the overlay follows it and has `z-index: 1001`. In Chrome the result is `close-button`, but in Obscura it is `loading-overlay`.

I found this while using CDP `Input.dispatchMouseEvent`: a coordinate click aimed at the visibly topmost close button was delivered to the lower-z overlay. Hit-testing should select the topmost painted element within the stacking context.

